\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\color black
Macroeconomic shocks affect sectors and regions with different intensities
 and delays.
 Population and economic activities tend to cluster in specific areas due
 economies of scale and agglomeration.
 The population tends to locate near the sea coast compared with interior
 continental areas.
 Sector activities tend to be concentrated in some areas or cities.
 Existence of a good economic infrastructure such as roads, airports, ports,
 and telecommunications facilities links regions economically and provides
 channels that can spread out economic shocks among regions.
 Macroeconomic models tend to disregard regional and spatial economic activity
 issues due to either their aggregate nature bias or complexity of theme.
 However for a specific region, it is important to understand how economic
 activity variance can be decomposed into macroeconomic and idiosyncratic
 factors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the past years, time series data at disaggregated and regional levels
 are becoming available.
 At the same time, efforts have been made in econometric literature to model
 high-dimensional problems.
 Two challenges must be faced to address interdependence among regions,
 and linkages from regions to macroeconomic scenario.
 The first is the curse of dimensionality.
 The second challenge involves modeling the linkage between regions.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chudik2011infinite"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 suggested two approaches to handle the curse of dimensionality: (i) shrinkage
 of the parameter space and (ii) shrinkage of the data.
 The authors dealt with the curse of dimensionality by shrinking the parameter
 space as the number of endogenous variables tends to infinity.
 In this configuration, the infinite-dimensional vector autoregressive model
 could be well approximated by a set of finite-dimensional small-scale models.
 Moreover, this model can be estimated separately in a consistent manner,
 which is the global vector autoregressive (GVAR) model proposed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "pesaran2004modeling"
literal "true"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore, this study proposes a novel approach for dealing with the curse
 of dimensionality when modeling the labor market: use of the GVAR methodology
 to model the labor market at a regional level, accounting for interdependence
 among regions as well as among global macroeconomic variables.
 The GVAR methodology eliminates the curse of dimensionality and provides
 a parsimonious model for the labor market, and since the all variables
 are treated as endogenous, the GVAR model allows the global variables to
 be affected by the regional variables through a feedback channel.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We use information collected by the Brazilian Institute of Geography and
 Statistics (IBGE)
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Initials come from 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Instituto Brasileiro de Geografia e Estatísticas
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset

 that maps infrastructure and economic linkages across all municipalities
 in Brazil to construct the weights used in GVAR to model interdependence
 across regions.
 We do not restrict our analysis to neighborhood effects on economic activity
 by constructing a measure of economic connections.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Focusing on the Brazilian labor market, the aim of study is twofold.
 First, we want to model the link between an aggregate macroeconomic variable
 and regional labor dynamics as well as accounts for interdependence among
 Brazilian regions.
 To achieve that, a GVAR methodology proposed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "pesaran2004modeling"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 is used.
 Second, we want to map how regional formal labor employment reacts to Brazilian
 national economic activity.
 We employ monthly records of admissions and discharges in the Brazilian
 formal regional labor market.
 The period of the study is only 2004 to 2016 due to data constraints.
 The model analysis led to the conclusion that the Brazilian Northeast and
 South regions are the most elastic, whereas the North and Central regions
 are less sensitive to macroeconomic shocks.
 The Southeast region ‒ the most developed in the country ‒ is at the middle
 level.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This study is divided into sections as follows: section 2 is a brief literature
 review of previous works related to the purpose of this study; section
 3 presents the methodology; section 4 defines the models used; section
 5 describes the data with a brief description of sources and characteristics;
 section 6 reports the results; and section 7 contains final considerations.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Literature Review
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This section presents a brief literature review on the literature that deals
 with spatial time series models and labor market.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Firstly, focusing on the Brazilian labor market, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "oliveira2001flutuaccoes"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 analyzed the fluctuations in the employment level of several Brazilian
 states compared to national employment.
 The purpose of their study was to verify whether it is possible to establish
 a long-term relationship between state employment and nationwide employment.
 To achieve this the authors applied a cointegration analysis, a methodology
 proposed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "engle1987co"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, as well as the model of unrestricted error correction as proposed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "pesaran1996testing"
literal "true"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The authors used a database that included a comprehensive coverage of the
 labor market in the Brazilian states, using data from the Annual Report
 on Social Information (RAIS)
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
 RAIS from the Portuguese Relação Anual de Informações Sociais.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 from 1985 through 1996.
 The results obtained from an Vector Error Correction model were consistent
 with those obtained with Engle and Granger method (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "engle1987co"
literal "true"

\end_inset

).
 The results lead to the conclusion that employment fluctuations in most
 states shares a common trend with national employment level.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The hypothesis that the formal labor market presents a different dynamics
 in Brazilian metropolitan and non-metropolitan areas was tested by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "kretzmann2009flutuaccoes"
literal "true"

\end_inset

.
 The goal of their study was to analyze the fluctuations in the labor market
 of metropolitan and non-metropolitan Brazilian areas.
 They used employment and unemployment data from the Ministry of Labor and
 Employment through the General Records of Employed and Unemployed Persons
 - CAGED
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Initials comes from 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\shape italic
Cadastro Geral de Empregados e Desempregados
\shape default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in Portuguese)
\end_layout

\end_inset

 - using monthly data from 1996 through 2007.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "kretzmann2009flutuaccoes"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 used cointegration analysis through the traditional method proposed by
 Engle-Granger, as well as the method proposed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "pesaran1996testing"
literal "true"

\end_inset

.
 The two methods produced the similar results, suggesting that the metropolitan
 and non-metropolitan areas are not co-integrated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
None of these paper deals with the curse of dimensionality.
 The authors opt to with pairs of series.
 The evidence that states and national share a common trend highlight a
 possible channel linking macro and regional dynamics.
 Evidence that metropolitan and non metropolitan areas do not share a common
 trend may be explained by the fact that neighborhood effects are not that
 relevant but when corrected by infrastructure connections they may be.
 This hypothesis is investigated in this work.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Secondly, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "pesaran2004modeling"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 introduced the Global Vector Autoregressive (GVAR) methodology by means
 of a study that modeled 11 different regions using data from 1979 through
 1999.
 This methodology was improved by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "dees2007exploring"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 with the collaboration of the European Central Bank.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One of the first studies that applied GVAR to tackle interdependence across
 regions is 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "schanne2015global"
literal "true"

\end_inset

.
 The author analyzed the regional labor market in Germany with a focus on
 forecasting regional labor markets.
 His study had two goals: the first one is to estimate a the GVAR model,
 as proposed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "pesaran2004modeling"
literal "true"

\end_inset

.
 Second, to use the developed GVAR model to construct a forecast for German
 employment accounting for spatial effects.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The GVAR model allows both the analysis of strong cross-section dependence
 and weak cross-section dependency.
 Consequently, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "schanne2015global"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 concludes that the data indicate semi-strong cross-section dependence and
 has consistent results since Germany is a polycentric economy, in contrast
 with the United Kingdom or France (places where there are clearly dominant
 regions).
 The final model used in his study is a basic GVAR specification, which
 is a model in first differences without imposing cointegration relations,
 and did not include a dominant region.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In his forecasting experiment, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "schanne2015global"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 estimated the basic GVAR model and GVAR models with leading indicators.
 The author used these models as a predictive forecasting tool for three
 and twelve months ahead – forecasting the level of unemployment in the
 regional divisions according to the federal employment agency of Germany.
 For comparison purposes, each indicator was estimated by a VAR in differences
 and a VAR in level.
 The accuracy of the prediction was evaluated by comparing the absolute
 mean of prediction errors (percentage) and by the Diebold-Mariano test
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "DM1995"
literal "true"

\end_inset

).
 Finally, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "schanne2015global"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 concluded that there is significant improvement in the accuracy of predictions
 when considering the interdependence between regions, emphasizing that
 the degree of improvement depends on the variables and horizon.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Methodology
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This section presents and describes the GVAR methodology, which is the main
 methodology used in this study.
 It is assumed that the reader is familiar with the VAR methodology and
 VECM as well as their terminologies.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chudik2011infinite"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 considered the curse of dimensionality in the case of large linear dynamic
 systems.
 To deal with the curse, they proposed to shrink the parameter space in
 the limit as the number of endogenous variables tends to infinity.
 They showed that by shrinking the parameter space, the infinite-dimensional
 VAR could be well approximated by a set of finite-dimensional small-scale
 models that can be consistently estimated separately, which is in the spirit
 of the GVAR models proposed in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "pesaran2004modeling"
literal "true"

\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chudik2011infinite"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 concluded that the GVAR approach can be used as an approximation to an
 nfinite dimension vector autoregressive model (IVAR) featuring all macroeconomi
c variables.
 This is true for stationary models as well as for systems with variables
 integrated of order one.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The GVAR model is a compact model that combines individual VECMs in which
 domestic variables are related to individual-specific foreign variables
 in a consistent manner.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Global vector autoregressive model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The GVAR methodology was originally proposed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "pesaran2004modeling"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 as a practical approach to construct a coherent global model.
 In the traditional VAR methodology, an unrestricted VAR(p) model with 
\shape italic
k
\shape default
 endogenous variables covering 
\shape italic
N
\shape default
 countries has a large number of unknown parameters of the order 
\begin_inset Formula $(k∙N)^{2}p+kN$
\end_inset

 in the mean plus 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{(k∙N+1)kN}{2}$
\end_inset

 in the variance, thus requiring a parsimonious solution.
 Therefore, the authors proposed the use of a model wherein, first, the
 error correction models specific to each region are estimated, with the
 domestic macroeconomic variables related to the corresponding foreign variables
, and second the models from each region are combined to generate forecasts
 for all variables.
 In short, the GVAR model combines the VECM models of each region, so that
 domestic variables are related to regionally specific foreign variables
 in a consistent manner.
 Therefore, the model can consider the interdependencies between economic
 regions in the analysis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Subsequently 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "dees2007exploring"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 improved the GVAR model with the cooperation of the European Central Bank.
 This version of the GVAR model included 26 countries (covering 90% of world
 production), with the Euro area being considered as one sole economy with
 estimates from 1979 to 2003.
 The version of GVAR model used in this study is based on that of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "dees2007exploring"
literal "true"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The GVAR model can be summarized as a two-step procedure.
 In the first step, specific models of each region are estimated that are
 conditioned to the external influences of other regions.
 That is, the models are represented as VAR models, which have domestic
 variables and foreign variables, the latter being treated as weakly exogenous.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "engle1983exogeneity"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 for a definition.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 In the second step, the VAR models of each region are grouped into one
 single GVAR model.
 The GVAR model, as well as any VAR model, can be used for analyzing shocks
 in the economy and making predictions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Specific models for each region
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the specific models used in the first stage of the GVAR methodology,
 consider a panel with 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 elements, each element showing 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{i}$
\end_inset

 variables observed during periods 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,2,\cdots,T$
\end_inset

.
 Within this panel structure the following variables are defined: 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i,t}$
\end_inset

 as a vector 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{i}×1$
\end_inset

 of domestic variables for element 
\shape italic
i
\shape default
 in period 
\shape italic
t
\shape default
; 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}^{*}$
\end_inset

 as a vector 
\begin_inset Formula $k^{*}×1$
\end_inset

 of foreign variables; and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i,t}$
\end_inset

 as an error vector.
 Thus, following the notation of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chudik2016theory"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, the specific models of each element consist of a set of domestic and foreign
 variables.
 Specifically, for any 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 element:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x_{i,t}=a_{i0}+a_{i1}t+\sum\limits _{j=1}^{p_{i}}\Phi_{ij}x_{i,t-j}+\Lambda_{i0}x_{i,t}^{*}+\sum\limits _{l=1}^{q_{i}}\Lambda_{il}x_{i,t-l}^{*}+u_{i,t},
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi_{ij}$
\end_inset

 is an array of lag coefficients for lag 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 associated with domestic variables, and 
\begin_inset Formula $Λ_{il}$
\end_inset

 is an array of lag coefficients for lag 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 associated with foreign variables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The GVAR model treats foreign variables as weights averages of the domestic
 variables of each corresponding element, and the weights are specific to
 each unit.
 Therefore, the foreign variables of each element 
\shape italic
i
\shape default
 can be computed as weights of the domestic variables of other units in
 the panel.
 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{ij}$
\end_inset

 is a weighting matrix, where 
\begin_inset Formula $j=1,2,\cdots,N$
\end_inset

 is defined as a set of weights with the following characteristics: 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{ii}=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{j=1}^{N}W_{ij}=1$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the foreign variables can be defined as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x_{i,t}^{*}=\sum\limits _{j=0}^{N}W_{ij}x_{jt}.\label{eq:W}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The weights are predetermined and used to capture the importance of region
 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 for the economy of region 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From the domestic and foreign variables estimated from the weighting previously
 mentioned, VECMs are estimated separately for each region, considering
 that in the VECMs, the foreign variables 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i,t}^{*}$
\end_inset

 are treated as weakly exogenous.
 The use of the VECMs allows cointegration between domestic variables and
 between domestic and foreign variables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Global model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Although estimation is performed individually, element by element, the GVAR
 model is solved by considering all variables as endogenous to the system.
 Thus, from the specific model of each element, 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{i,t}$
\end_inset

 is defined as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
Z_{i,t}=\begin{bmatrix}x_{i,t}\\
x_{i,t}^{*}
\end{bmatrix},\label{eq:ZZ}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:W"

\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:ZZ"

\end_inset

 it is possible to obtain
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
A_{i0}Z_{i,t}=a_{i0}+a_{i1}t+\sum_{l=1}^{p}A_{il}Z_{i,t-l}+ε_{i,t},\label{eq:AZ}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $p=\max_{i}\{p,q_{i}\}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{i0}=[\begin{array}{cc}
I_{k_{i}} & -\Lambda_{i0}\end{array}]$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{il}=[\begin{array}{cc}
\Phi_{il} & \Lambda_{il}\end{array}]$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $l=1,2,⋯,p$
\end_inset

.
 Moreover we define 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi_{il}=0$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $l>p_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda_{il}=0$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $l>q_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}=[x_{0,t}^{'},x_{1,t}^{'},\cdots,x_{N,t}^{'},]^{'}$
\end_inset

 be a vector 
\begin_inset Formula $k×1$
\end_inset

 of all the variables in the panel, where 
\begin_inset Formula $k=∑_{i=1}^{N}k_{i}$
\end_inset

 .
 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{i}$
\end_inset

 can be defined as a matrix of dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $(k_{i}+k_{i}^{*})×1$
\end_inset

 of known constants defined in terms of the individual weights 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{ij}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, variables of the specific models are defined as a function of
 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
Z_{i,t}=W_{i}x_{t}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using this identity, the individual models can be written as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
A_{i0}W_{i}x_{t}=a_{i0}+a_{i1}t+\sum_{l=1}^{p}A_{il}W_{i}x_{t-l}+ε_{i,t},
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is possible to combine the new individual models into one single model,
 and this can be done by “stacking up” each model in a large-scale VAR.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
G_{0}x_{t}=a_{0}+a_{1}t+\sum_{l=1}^{p}G_{l}x_{t-l}+ε_{t},\label{eq:g0xt}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{ccccc}
G_{0}=\begin{bmatrix}A_{00}W_{0}\\
A_{10}W_{1}\\
\vdots\\
A_{N0}W_{N}
\end{bmatrix}, & G_{1}=\begin{bmatrix}A_{0l}W_{0}\\
A_{1l}W_{1}\\
\vdots\\
A_{Nl}W_{N}
\end{bmatrix}, & a_{0}=\begin{bmatrix}a_{00}\\
a_{10}\\
\vdots\\
a_{N0}
\end{bmatrix}, & a_{1}=\begin{bmatrix}a_{01}\\
a_{11}\\
\vdots\\
a_{N1}
\end{bmatrix}, & \varepsilon_{t}=\begin{bmatrix}\varepsilon_{0,t}\\
\varepsilon_{1,t}\\
\vdots\\
\varepsilon_{N,t}
\end{bmatrix},\end{array}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since the 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{0}$
\end_inset

 matrix is a non-singular matrix, which depends only on the estimated parameters
 and the link matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{i}$
\end_inset

, it is possible to rewrite equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:g0xt"

\end_inset

) as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x_{t}=b_{0}+b_{1}t+\sum_{i=1}^{p}F_{i}x_{t-i}+\epsilon_{t},
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{c}
F_{i}=G_{0}^{-1}G_{i},\\
b_{0}=G_{0}^{-1}a_{0},\\
b_{1}=G_{0}^{-1}a_{1},\\
\epsilon_{t}=G_{0}^{-1}ε_{t},
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Hence, the global model, which consists of a set of individual models from
 each region, can be solved recursively.
 With that, it is possible to consistently determine the coefficients of
 the model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Dominant unit model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Global VAR is based on the hypothesis of weak exogeneity (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "engle1983exogeneity"
literal "true"

\end_inset

).
 If this hypothesis fails for one or more regions, these regions must be
 modeled jointly.
 In spatial analysis, it is possible that some important regions affect
 others in a specific form.
 This information must be included in the model.
 Therefore, vector 
\begin_inset Formula $ω_{t}$
\end_inset

 of common variables and its lagged values augment each specific model.
 So, each element of the model can be stated as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x_{i,t}=a_{i0}+a_{i1}t+\sum_{j=1}^{p_{i}}\Phi_{ij}x_{i,t-j}+\Lambda_{i0}x_{i,t}^{*}+\sum_{l=1}^{q_{i}}\Lambda_{il}x_{i,t-l}^{*}+D_{i0}ω_{t}+\sum_{l=1}^{s_{i}}Ψ_{il}ω_{t-l}+u_{i,t},
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The marginal model for the dominant variables can be estimated with the
 feedback effects from 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}$
\end_inset

, or not.
 The feedback from the variables in the GVAR model are captured via cross-sectio
n averages, and this is done by augmenting the dominant unit model with
 lags of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ωt}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
ω_{t}=\sum_{l=1}^{p_{ω}}\Phi_{ωl}ω_{t-l}+\sum_{l=1}^{q_{ω}}\Lambda_{ωl}x_{i,t-l}^{*}+\eta_{ωt}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Different lag orders for the dominant variables (
\begin_inset Formula $p_{ω}$
\end_inset

) and cross-section averages (
\begin_inset Formula $q_{ω}$
\end_inset

) could be considered.
 It is worthy to point out that contemporaneous values of foreign variables
 do not feature in the equation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Regional weights
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally, the number of connections between regions were obtained from 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ibge"
literal "true"

\end_inset

.
 This work aims to determine economic linkage among municipalities in Brazil.
 The study analyzed not only regular transport links, such as those that
 go to the urban centers, but also the main destinations of the residents
 to obtain products and services, such as general goods, education, airport
 travel, and health services.
 The acquisition of agricultural inputs and destination of the agricultural
 products were also analyzed in the study.
 The main result of the research is a Brazilian urban network map that connects
 all 5,564 Brazilian municipalities.
 This map contains the hierarchy of the urban network and the regions of
 influence of each urban center.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A chart indicating the connections between all Brazilian cities is constructed
 by merging the links provided in the urban network framework, which are
 the main links between municipalities, with the links regarding airport
 connections, shopping destinations, education, health services, and recreation
 activities.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since every city belongs to a mesoregion, the links between mesoregions
 are also determined with the following characteristics: if the two cities
 are within the same region then the link between them is an internal link;
 if the two cities are in different mesoregions, then the link between them
 is a connection between the two mesoregions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As mentioned earlier, the weight matrix is a function of the number of connectio
ns between regions; however at this point, it is worth mentioning that some
 connections are more important than others.
 Moreover, since the measurement of importance for each connection between
 each region is unfeasible, an equal weight to all connections is assumed,
 resulting in a linear function, which is the average.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The weight matrix to region 
\shape italic
i
\shape default
 can then be determined by the number of connections to other regions divided
 by its total number of connections.
 Therefore, the weight vector for each region is determined as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
W_{i}=\frac{\begin{bmatrix}C_{i1} & \cdots & C_{iN}\end{bmatrix}}{\sum_{j=1}^{N}C_{ij}},\label{eq:dddd}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{ij}$
\end_inset

 is the number of connections that originated at region 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and have region 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 as destination, keeping in mind that connections within the same region
 (
\begin_inset Formula $i=j$
\end_inset

), also called internal links, have a zero weight (
\begin_inset Formula $C_{ii}=0$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One most important step in GVAR methodology is the construction of the weight
 matrix.
 In Pesaran’s initial work, the weight matrix is defined by country-specific
 trade weights.
 In fact, the weight matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{i}$
\end_inset

 is designed to capture the importance of all other regions for region 
\shape italic
i
\shape default
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealp
key "di2013gvar"
literal "true"

\end_inset

).
 So, to determine the weight matrix it is necessary to determine the importance
 of each region in respect to every other region.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Focusing on the labor market, to determine the importance of a region to
 another is to determine the importance of one labor market to another labor
 market.
 Each region has its own labor market with firms and workers, and since
 firms cannot change markets, it is the workers that freely change markets.
 In other words, a worker can look for a job in other regions, but a firm
 must attract workers from other regions to its own market.
 Consequently, for a worker to commute to another region, there must be
 a connection between these regions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The connections between regions go beyond neighborhood effects in an integrated
 economy.
 There are many other channels such as physical infrastructure that allow
 workers to commute (by car, by train, by plane, etc.) to another region.
 Therefore, regions with higher connections are more important than regions
 with fewer connections to model a specific unit.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consequently, the importance of region 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 to region 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 will be a function of the connections between them, and the weight matrix
 will be a based on the number of connections between the regions.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Database Description and Model Specification 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The labor market interacts with the macroeconomic environment through many
 channels.
 One of these channels is the level of economic activity.
 Industrial production is used as a proxy to economic activity.
 It is highly correlated to the gross domestic product (GDP) but available
 at a higher frequency level.
 The first is available quarterly whereas the latter is available monthly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally, an attempt to model the Brazilian labor market at the municipality
 level was made but then discarded.
 Brazil had a total of 5,564 municipalities in 2005 according to IBGE.
 This government institute grouped municipalities into 137 mesoregions and
 27 states.
 Many municipalities were merged or split, and thus it is difficult to construct
 a long time series.
 Therefore, we opt to work at the mesoregional level.
\end_layout

