\begin_layout Standard
Data on each mesoregion are grouped into five Brazilian regions: North,
 Northeast, Southeast, South and Centralwest.
 Taking the IBGE's estimated population for 2016 as the total population,
 and 0% growth as a base scenario, it is possible to obtain the industrial
 production elasticity of employment for each major region over an eight-year
 forecast period.
 Considering a 2% annual growth in the industrial production, the South
 region is the most sensitive with 0.18 elasticity; the second most sensitive
 region is the Northeast with 0.15 elasticity.
 The Southeast region has 0.10 elasticity, while the least sensitive major
 regions are the North and Centralwest, each with 0.06 elasticity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This indicates that the Northeast and South regions have higher elasticity,
 being strongly affected by changes in industrial production.
 The Southeast region, which is the most developed, has intermediate elasticity.
 This is probably due to the high development level of the region, making
 its marginal gain not as high as in the Northeast and South regions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Although previous analysis was conducted in macroregions, the GVAR methodology
 allows us to conduct the same analysis at a more in-depth level.
 Since the GVAR equations are solved for every region, we can evaluate elasticit
y for not only the state but also for every mesoregion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
